  <h2 id="answer">The scope and salary answer, upfront</h2>

  <p>
    Physiotherapy has real, growing scope in India — an aging population, a rising sports and fitness culture, higher post-surgical rehab volume, and a fast-expanding home-healthcare segment are all pushing demand up. But the salary story most articles tell (a single "average salary" figure) hides the part that actually matters for your decision: which employer track you are on changes your income more than your degree does.
  </p>

  <p>
    A physiotherapist working in a small private clinic in a tier-2 city and one working inside a corporate hospital chain in Mumbai or Bangalore are not on comparable income paths in year one, even with an identical BPT degree. The rest of this guide breaks that apart by structure, specialization, and employer type so you can plan around the real numbers, not a blended average.
  </p>

  <h2 id="structure">BPT and MPT: the real course structure</h2>

  <p>
    The Bachelor of Physiotherapy (BPT) is the entry-level professional degree, regulated under the National Commission for Allied and Healthcare Professions (NCAHP). It runs 4.5 to 5 years total: four years of academic and clinical coursework — biomedical science foundations in year one, movement and therapy fundamentals in year two, system-specific physiotherapy in year three, and specialised applied physiotherapy in year four — followed by a compulsory one-year rotatory internship of at least 2,016 clinical hours.
  </p>

  <p>
    A Master of Physiotherapy (MPT) is a further 2-year postgraduate degree pursued after BPT, letting you specialise in orthopedics, neurology, sports physiotherapy, pediatrics, cardiopulmonary physiotherapy, or musculoskeletal physiotherapy. An MPT is not mandatory to practise, but it meaningfully improves your access to senior clinical roles, teaching positions, sports-team roles, and higher pay bands in corporate hospitals.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Most guides present BPT-then-MPT as an automatic next step. It is not automatic — it is a real cost and time decision. If you are aiming for general clinical practice or a government-hospital role, a strong BPT plus hands-on clinical experience can be enough. If you specifically want sports physiotherapy, neuro rehab leadership, or teaching, the MPT is close to a requirement, not an optional upgrade.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">This is a licensed profession, not a self-taught skill</p>
    <p class="callout__footer" style="margin-top:0;">
      Unlike many high-income skills you can build through YouTube courses or self-study, physiotherapy is a formally gated clinical field. You need a BPT (or equivalent) degree from an NCAHP-recognised institution, the compulsory supervised internship, and registration with your State Physiotherapy Council or the national body once it is operational, before you can legally practise or use the title "physiotherapist" in India. No amount of online learning substitutes for this — treat any course or platform that suggests otherwise as a red flag.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="neet-status">The NEET question for 2026-27</h2>

  <p>
    This is the part of physiotherapy admissions most articles either skip or get outdated on fast, so it deserves a direct answer.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Current status, verify before you rely on it</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>NCAHP originally proposed making NEET-UG the common entrance exam for BPT and BOT (Bachelor of Occupational Therapy) admissions starting the 2026-27 academic year.</li>
      <li>After pushback from institutions, students, and professional bodies including the Indian Association of Physiotherapists, NCAHP deferred the NEET-mandatory requirement to the 2027-28 academic year.</li>
      <li>For the 2026-27 admission cycle, most institutions are admitting through a merit list based on aggregate Class 12 Physics, Chemistry, and Biology marks (commonly a minimum 50% aggregate), not NEET scores.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      This is a year-specific administrative decision, not a settled rule the way NEET-for-MBBS is settled. Confirm the exact current-year requirement directly on the NCAHP portal or your target college's official admission notice before you plan a full academic year around it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="specializations">Sports, neuro, ortho, pediatric: the 4 tracks that matter</h2>

  <p>
    "Physiotherapist" is not one job. The daily work, patient relationships, and pay pattern differ meaningfully by track.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
