        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Cardiopulmonary physiotherapy (working with cardiac and respiratory patients, often in ICU or post-cardiac-surgery settings) is a fifth real track worth knowing about — smaller in volume than the four above, but consistently in demand inside hospital ICU and cardiac-rehab units.
  </p>

  <p>
    Registration is not a one-time event either. Most State Physiotherapy Councils expect periodic renewal, and increasingly ask for evidence of continuing professional development (CPD) — workshops, certification courses, or conference credits — to keep your registration active. Build a light CPD habit early (one certification or workshop a year in your chosen track) rather than treating registration as a formality you handle once and forget.
  </p>

  <p>
    International demand is a real, if smaller, sixth path worth knowing about: countries like the UK (NHS), Canada, Australia, and the Gulf region regularly recruit Indian-trained physiotherapists, though each requires separate licensing exams (such as the UK's HCPC route or Canada's PCE) on top of your BPT/MPT — treat this as a multi-year plan, not a quick add-on.
  </p>

